# Copy to .env.perf and fill in values
# This should match your production .env EXCEPT the data volumes are different (handled by docker-compose)

# SESSION_SECRET and SUPERADMIN_PASSWORD are auto-generated (see server log for superadmin password)
BASE_URL=https://your-production-url.example.com

# Keep NODE_ENV=production for realistic HTTPS cookie behavior
NODE_ENV=production

# v8.8.26 / v8.8.27 — REQUIRED, both of them, with NO defaults. The server exits
# FATAL at startup without either.
#
# They used to default to `<repo>/data` and `<repo>/uploads`, which on a
# developer's machine were symlinks to the LIVE deployment's volumes — and the
# server opens the database and SWEEPS the uploads directory at boot, so a perf
# run that inherited those defaults would have written to, and deleted from, real
# data. `perf/run-local.sh` has always set both to throwaway paths under
# `perf/.local/`; this file is for the OTHER way of running a target (a container
# or a host you point `--base-url` at), where nothing sets them for you.
#
# Two SEPARATE filesystems by design — the upload capacity check measures free
# space on both (v7.15.12) — so do not point them at one directory.
#
# In a container these are the paths INSIDE it (docker-compose mounts the host
# volumes onto them). On a host, use absolute paths.
DATA_DIR=/app/data
UPLOADS_DIR=/app/uploads

# SMTP — REQUIRED as of v6.x. The server exits FATAL at startup without
# SMTP_HOST. For perf runs, point it at a capture server (mailpit,
# smtp4dev, maildev) or any SMTP endpoint — the mail content is
# irrelevant to the benchmark. Example for a mailpit container on the
# same host:
#   SMTP_HOST=mailpit
#   SMTP_PORT=1025
SMTP_HOST=mailpit
SMTP_PORT=1025
# Only the ADDRESS half is used (v7.26.0) — the sender NAME comes from
# Settings -> Platform Name.
EMAIL_FROM=no-reply@hackathon.local

# v7.15.34 — RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED is deliberately ABSENT.
#
# It used to be set here, and because env_file applies for the container's
# whole life that meant the MEASUREMENT never saw rate limiting either — so
# CTF-0026 (per-(IP,email) login buckets + the per-IP backstop) was excluded
# from every perf run ever done.
#
# The stated reason was the 10/min /api/register cap. That rationale died in
# v6.10.80, when the seeder switched to POST /api/admin/perf/bulk-seed-users:
# seed.js now calls /api/register ZERO times. Of its ~380 requests only six
# touch a limited route, each under 2% of its per-minute budget, and the heavy
# phases (bulk-seed-users, bulk-seed-history, the task-create loop) carry no
# limiter at all. Measured on a medium profile: 2s with the limiter off, 3s
# with it on — clean DB and dirty re-seed alike.
#
# So do not re-add it. If some future seeder change ever does need it (the
# candidates are the shared 30/min destructive-admin bucket, or a 4xx burst
# tripping the scanner-reputation ban), set it for the SEED phase only and
# restart the container without it before measuring.

# Reverse-proxy trust. Leave OFF unless the perf container really does
# sit behind a proxy. When enabled, the server honours
# X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto.
# TRUST_PROXY=1
